\label{Prop:BiellipticGenus1}
Let $[f \colon \widetilde{C} \to C] \in \mathcal{R}_{B,g,0}$.  If $r \geq 2$, then $V^r (C,f) = Y_{r+1}$.
As a consequence, $V^r (C,f)$ has dimension $g-r-2$ when $g-r-2 \geq 0$ and is empty otherwise.  If $g>r+2$, then $V^r (C,f)$ is irreducible, and if $g=r+2$, then $V^r (C,f)$ consists of two points.

\label{Thm:PrymZonotope}
Let $\Gamma$ be a bielliptic loop of loops and $\pi \colon \widetilde{\Gamma} \to \Gamma$ the quotient by the antipodal involution.  If $r \geq 2$, then $V^r (\Gamma,\pi)$ is a union of tori, and the intersection lattice of these tori is the face lattice of a zonotope. 

In the special case where $r=2$, the statement holds for every loop of loops $\Gamma$, regardless of edge lengths.

\cite[Corollary~5.2.8]{Debarre}
\label{Lem:Simple}
If $D_2$ is $p_2$-simple, then
\[
h^0 (\widetilde{C}, f_1^* D_1 + f_2^* D_2) \leq 2h^0 (C_1, D_1) + g(C_1) - 1 - \deg (D_1) .
\]

\cite[Lemma~4.8 and Proposition~4.20]{PBN}
\label{Lem:Contains}
For $Q, Q' \in P(r+1, g-1)$, we have $T(Q) \subseteq T(Q')$ if and only if $Q \leq Q'$.  Moreover, the intersection $T(Q) \cap T(Q')$ is nonempty if and only if $Q$ and $Q'$ have a common lower bound, in which case $T(Q) \cap T(Q') = T(Q \wedge Q')$.

\cite[Proposition~2.2.2]{OrientedMatroids}
\label{Prop:ZonotopeMatroid}
Let $\vec{x}_1 , \ldots , \vec{x}_n \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and let $M$ be the corresponding oriented matroid.  There is an order-reversing bijection between the face lattice of the zonotope $Z (\vec{x}_1 , \ldots , \vec{x}_n)$ and the lattice of covectors of $M$.

\label{Thm:PrymZonotope}
Let $\Gamma$ be a bielliptic loop of loops and $\pi \colon \widetilde{\Gamma} \to \Gamma$ the quotient by the antipodal involution.  If $r \geq 2$, then $V^r (\Gamma,\pi)$ is a union of tori, and the intersection lattice of these tori is the face lattice of a zonotope. 

In the special case where $r=2$, the statement holds for every loop of loops $\Gamma$, regardless of edge lengths.

\label{Ex:RhombicIcosahedron}
We illustrate Theorem~\ref{Thm:PrymZonotope} in the first case where the Prym-Brill-Noether variety is two-dimensional, when $g=6$ and $r=2$.  In this case, $V^2 (\Gamma,\pi)$ is a union of tori $T(Q)$, each of dimension 2, 1, or 0.  The poset $P(3,5)$ is the face lattice of the \emph{rhombic icosahedron}, pictured in Figure~\ref{Fig:RhombicIcosahedron}.  In the figure, each of the 20 2-dimensional faces is labeled with a lingering word $Q$ in the symbols 0, 1, and $\epsilon$, corresponding to the 2-dimensional torus $T(Q)$.  When the faces labeled $Q$ and $Q'$ meet along an edge, it means that the intersection $T(Q) \cap T(Q')$ is a circle.  Similarly, the 22 vertices, which correspond to the 22 words of length 5 in the symbols 0 and 1 that contain a length-3 alternating subword, also correspond to points where two or more of the 1-dimensional tori intersect.

